{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Then the priest shall command, and he shall take, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory in Torat Kohanim said: the commanding is by the priest, and the taking is by any person. And they expounded in the verse after this one, ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְשָׁחַט״ — the commanding is by the priest, and the slaughtering is by any person; these are the words of Rabbi Yehudah bar Rabbi Yossi. Rabbi says: even the slaughtering is by the priest, end of discussion. It is difficult: why did Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi disagree only regarding the second verse and not the first? For if it is because in ״וְצִוָּה וְלָקַח״ there is nothing to superimpose upon the commanding alone, to the exclusion of the taking — similarly in ״וְצִוָּה וְשָׁחַט״ it is unknown what there is in the commanding alone to the exclusion of the slaughtering. And on the contrary, in the commanding of ״וְלָקַח״ we can say that the intention is that ״וְצִוָּה״ means they should bring the birds before him and he himself should take them from the hand of those who bring them, so that two commandments would need to be performed by the priest — whereas in ״וְצִוָּה וְשָׁחַט,״ if we say that the priest is the one who should slaughter, with what do we superimpose the commanding?" "<b>And I saw</b> that the author of Korban Aharon wrote that the commandment is that he should command to bring the two birds before him, to select from them the one that is designated and chosen. And the reason that Rabbi did not disagree regarding the first verse is because he did not find anything upon which to superimpose the commandment, end of discussion. And his words do not appear correct, for this commandment of selection — which would need to be in the hand of a priest — is not heard from the statement ״וְצִוָּה.״ And on the contrary, we should say that the intention of the verse is to say that he should command to select the one, and the verse further intends to say that the commandment of selection is through the priest, while the selection is through someone else, and the slaughtering is through the priest — and in any case, Rabbi could interpret the first verse in the same way he interpreted the second verse." "And it would appear preferable to explain that the reason Rabbi disagreed and held that the slaughtering too is by the priest is not from the implication of the verse, but from the nature of the matter itself. For HaShem commanded that the slaughter be into an earthen vessel over living water, and this slaughter is not merely an act of slaughter, since it is accompanied by another act — that one slaughter into a vessel, etc. — and this is the primary preparation of the purification. Therefore he said that it must be done through a priest. Whereas in the first verse, the taking of the birds involves no act of purification, and the Torah left the matter to the Sages to distinguish among the things." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to say that in any case the plain sense of the verse, when it says ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְלָקַח וְגוֹ׳,״ its intention in saying ״וְלָקַח״ is about the priest, for the verse has no need to mention the priest regarding each and every thing. For what would you say — if it said ״וְצִוָּה וְלָקַח הַכֹּהֵן,״ then we would say through proper analysis that the commanding is not about the priest, because its mention comes at the end. But since it said ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ — mentioning the priest at the beginning — whatever action follows afterward refers back to the one mentioned earlier. From this point, when the first verse says ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְלָקַח,״ the plain sense of the verse is that the priest shall command and he shall take. But from the fact that the verse afterward says ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְשָׁחַט,״ this points with a finger that what the verse said, ״וְלָקַח,״ does not refer back to the priest — for if the verse is speaking about the priest all along, why was it necessary to say ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ a second time? Is it not speaking about the priest all along, so it should have said only ״וְצִוָּה וְשָׁחַט״ and I would know it speaks about the priest? Rather, surely it cut off to someone else, and who is that? The ״וְלָקַח״ that preceded it, which speaks about any person. Therefore, according to everyone — both Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi — they hold that in the first verse, ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְלָקַח,״ means any person, whereas the second verse, where we have no necessity [to say it means a non-priest], Rabbi holds that the verse stands according to its plain sense: ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְשָׁחַט״ — the priest as well, the one adjacent to it." "And Rabbi Yehudah holds ״his friend may inform him״ — once it was revealed to us from the first verse that the word ״וְלָקַח״ does not refer back to the adjacent priest, likewise the second verse is interpreted in the same manner as the first." "<b>Rather,</b> I saw that they expounded there in Torat Kohanim at the beginning of the parashah, and this is their language: ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — בְּכֹהֵן, etc. I have only his purification and his impurity requiring a priest; from where do I derive the slaughter of the birds and the sprinkling, etc., and his shaving? Scripture says: ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — בְּכֹהֵן. Might one say even the taking of the birds and the sending of the birds and the washing, etc., also require a priest? Scripture says: ״זֹאת״ — end of quote. Now, from the plain sense of the baraita it appears that there is no need for a priest except for the three specifics enumerated precisely, and if so, there is no need for the priest to give orders — neither concerning the taking nor concerning the slaughter. And from both baraitot it is explicitly clear to all, whether according to Rabbi Yehudah or according to Rabbi, that the priest must command two things." "It may be said that the Tanna did not mention the two command-acts in the baraita along with the three things, for the reason that in the exposition of ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ there are included only matters that belong to the substance of the purification — for the word ״זֹאת״ excludes many acts, and were it not for the phrase ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ the command-acts would also be excluded. Therefore he mentioned only the particulars derived from the phrase ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״, but the command-acts — this is not their place; rather, the verse set them apart in their own place and said ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ etc., and the Tanna relied on what he would later expound in the section of ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ — that the command is by the priest." "<b>Rather,</b> I saw that the Rambam (Hilkhot Tumat Tzara'at, chapter 11, halakhah 5) wrote that nothing requires a priest except the slaughter of the birds and the sprinkling and the shaving, and he did not mention at all the matter of command-acts that must be performed by a priest. And on the contrary, he stated a general principle and said: וּשְׁאָר הַדְּבָרִים — whether by a priest or by an Israelite. And although in the Tosefta in chapter 8 of Nega'im it is taught in accordance with the Rambam's words — that nothing requires a priest except three things, and the rest of the matters by any person, and there too the matter of command by a priest is not mentioned — we should not presumptuously establish the Tosefta as following no one's opinion; and we may say further that the Tanna explained the matters in another place, and also that it is explicit in the Torah that the command is by the priest and there was no need to state it. But this is not the case with the Rambam, who needed to explain the matter, and it is not so explicit in the Torah that we are forced to say that even the Rambam had no need to write it — for we see that the Tannaim needed to derive the matter from Scripture." "And one cannot say that the Rambam holds that the plain sense of the Tosefta — which said ״וּשְׁאָר הַדְּבָרִים בְּכָל אָדָם״, implying even the command-acts — means that it follows neither Rabbi Yehudah nor Rabbi. If we were to say so, a further difficulty arises, for it is a simple matter that one need not be concerned about a Tosefta such as this that follows neither Rabbi Yehudah nor Rabbi, whose words were cited in the Sifra Debei Rav, the refined work." "And it is possible to say that the Rambam holds this by force of the first baraita in Torat Kohanim, which mentioned the matters requiring a priest in the exposition of the verse ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״ — that all of those matters are Torah-law requirements for validity (le'ikkuvah). And the exposition of ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן״ follows this path: that the priest is to command that the matters be done — and this is a matter of honor, that the actions be carried out at his word and by his directive, in the manner of ״וְעַל פִּיךָ יִשַּׁק כָּל עַמִּי״ (Genesis 41:40). And this is what the verse means by the command being upon the priest — to decree the matter while others perform it, and the implication of the language indicates as much, that this is not required for validity but is an honor for the priest. And furthermore, the matter follows an a fortiori argument: since concerning the sending of the live bird, etc., Scripture states that it is not a requirement for validity (as it is written ״וְשִׁלַּח אֶת הַצִּפֹּר הַחַיָּה״ and the bird may be sent by anyone), then certainly the taking and the slaughter are not required for validity. And Rabbi, who said that even the slaughter must be performed by a priest — this is not derived from the force of the verse ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וְשָׁחַט״, but from the force of the exposition of ״תּוֹרַת הַמְּצֹרָע״, that the slaughter too is like the sprinkling and the shaving; and he is the first Tanna who included the slaughter together with the shaving and the sprinkling."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:57:08.682376019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}